1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:02,874
Chapter six. A window in the living room.

2
00:00:03,224 --> 00:00:10,993
Yet RCA took the spotlight: at the New York World's Fair, Sarnoff unveiled television to the public as if it were his own.

3
00:00:11,193 --> 00:00:21,910
The war put everything on hold. But afterwards, television exploded. In the United States, in 1950, barely one in ten homes had one.

4
00:00:22,110 --> 00:00:29,433
Ten years later, nearly nine out of ten. Across Latin America, the first broadcasts also arrived during the 1950s.

5
00:00:29,883 --> 00:00:36,581
And how do we see motion? If you show the eye many still pictures per second, the brain blends them together.

6
00:00:36,781 --> 00:00:43,435
A tube television drew more than five hundred lines per picture. And between twenty-five and thirty pictures every second.

7
00:00:43,635 --> 00:00:49,797
That is why, if you film an old TV with your phone, you see dark bands: the camera catches it halfway through drawing.

8
00:00:49,997 --> 00:01:01,294
Then came color: three tiny dots—red, green, and blue—that mix to form every other color. Your screen still uses those three today.

9
00:01:02,194 --> 00:01:07,062
Families no longer gathered around the radio. They gathered in front of a window.
